<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wissembourg Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Weissenburg Abbey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wissembourg Abbey was a Benedictine monastery in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> on the river Lauter, founded in the 7th century and dedicated to Saints Peter and Paul. Richly endowed by Frankish kings, it was a centre of learning where the monk Otfrid wrote his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-high-german">Old High German</a> Gospel Harmony in the 9th century. The town of Wissembourg grew around it, and its Gothic church survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wissembourg-abbey.jpg" alt="Wissembourg Abbey" width="320" height="521" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frank · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Weissenburg-St-Pierre-et-St-Paul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
